HC="No health or brainwave-entrainment claims are made by this skill; it is a sound-construction recipe only. Keep pulse/beat layers at low listening level."
mk("WNewLv-kI2I","How to Create Isochronic Tones in Ableton Live with Huon Studio",
 "isochronic-tone-layer-under-music-sine-carrier-midi-note-amp-pulse-blend-smooth-step-shape-fade-in-out-automate-pulse-rate-15-to-4-hz-and-back-10ms-smoothing-carrier-tuning-432-or-custom-map",
 "Add an isochronic pulse layer under an existing track: a sine (or noise) carrier played by one long MIDI note, amplitude-pulsed at a chosen rate (e.g. 4 Hz) with a smooth-step pulse shape and a 0–100% isochronic blend, faded in and out by gain automation, with the pulse rate automated (e.g. 15 Hz → 4 Hz → 15 Hz) using ~10 ms parameter smoothing, carrier tuned by reference (440/432) or a custom note-to-frequency map and verified with a tuner, then mixed barely audible",
 "Build the Mind Amend-style isochronic layer as its own track. A tone generator plays a sine carrier from MIDI; with beat frequency 0 it is just a plain sine. Pick the pulse rate (e.g. 4 Hz) and raise the isochronic blend from 0% (steady tone) through 50% to 100% (full on/off pulses); choose a smooth-step pulse shape for focused but soft pulses. An amplitude-pulsing (LFO/tremolo) device lets you pulse noise or any audio as well. Use one long MIDI note for an uninterrupted tone (chords also work and pulse together). Fade in over about 30 s and out over 30–60 s with gain automation. Automate the pulse rate with exact typed breakpoints (e.g. start 15 Hz, glide to 4 Hz, return to 15 Hz at the end) and set the device's parameter smoothing to its minimum (0.01 = 10 ms) for automation. Keep pulse rates low (the speaker suggests below ~100 Hz); carrier frequencies (e.g. 432 Hz reference tuning, or a custom mapping where a note equals 528 Hz) are a separate layer of choice; check the actual pitch with a tuner set to the same reference. Finally set the layer's level in the mixer so it sits barely audible under the music. Polyrhythm Studio: OscillatorNode → GainNode whose gain follows a pulse-shaped LFO at rate r(t) with a ramped automation curve.",
 ["DAW with a tone/isochronic generator or any synth + tremolo/LFO amplitude device, gain utility with automation, and a tuner, or Web Audio: OscillatorNode (sine) → GainNode; gain driven by a smoothed square/raised-cosine LFO whose frequency AudioParam is automated with linearRampToValueAtTime (implementation note)","An existing music track to sit under"],
 [("carrier","number|note","Carrier pitch (MIDI note, or Hz via tuning reference/custom map)",True),("pulse_rate","number|curve","Pulse rate in Hz (e.g. 4) or an automation curve (15 → 4 → 15 Hz)",True),("blend","number","Isochronic blend 0–100%",True),("pulse_shape","enum","Pulse shape (smooth step recommended in the video)",False),("fades","object","Fade-in (~30 s) and fade-out (30–60 s) times",False)],
 [("isochronic_layer","track","Pulsed carrier track with fades and rate automation"),("mix_level","number","Final layer level relative to the music")],
 [HC,"Beat/pulse rate (a few Hz) and carrier frequency (hundreds of Hz) are different parameters; don't type a carrier value into the pulse rate.","With beat frequency 0 or blend 0% nothing is pulsed.","Set parameter smoothing to its minimum before automating rate, or fast changes lag.","Specific note-to-frequency maps (e.g. 'solfeggio' tunings) are user-supplied tuning tables; verify each pitch with a tuner rather than trusting the label. Product names mapped to generic tone generator + amplitude LFO."],
 [("Create a MIDI track with the tone generator; set the device output low (about -20 dB) so the layer starts quiet.","setup","A plain sine carrier at low level"),
  ("Program one long MIDI note (or loop a short note while designing; chords also work).","carrier note","A steady carrier tone without interruptions"),
  ("Set the pulse rate (e.g. 4 Hz) and raise isochronic blend from 0% to 100%; choose a smooth-step pulse shape.","pulse","The tone turns on and off 4 times per second with soft edges"),
  ("Add a gain utility and automate a ~30 s fade-in at the start and a 30–60 s fade-out at the end.","fades","The pulsed tone fades in and out gently"),
  ("Expose the pulse-rate parameter for automation, set parameter smoothing to 0.01 (10 ms), and draw breakpoints with typed values: 15 Hz at the start, 4 Hz in the middle section, back to 15 Hz at the end.","rate automation","Pulses audibly slow from fast to 4 Hz and speed up again"),
  ("Optional carrier tuning: switch the tuning reference (440 → 432 Hz) or load a custom note-to-frequency map and find the note that gives the target frequency (e.g. 528 Hz); confirm with a tuner set to the same reference.","carrier tuning","The tuner confirms the intended carrier pitch"),
  ("Set the track fader so the layer is barely audible under the music.","mix","Pulses are perceptible but stay in the background")],
 ["At blend 0% the output is a steady sine; at 100% it is fully gated at the pulse rate.","Counting pulses over 1 s at the 4 Hz section gives about 4.","The rate automation lane shows 15 → 4 → 15 Hz with exact typed values.","A tuner shows the intended carrier frequency."],
 ["isochronic","pulse","amplitude-modulation","meditation","ambient","automation","carrier","tuning","mind-amend","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Two-person screen-share session; off-topic chat dropped; product UI mapped to generic tone generator + amplitude pulse.")

mk("GndBM5EifHA","Producing a Binaural Beats Meditation Track in Ableton Live (Step-By-Step)",
 "binaural-beat-meditation-track-pad-foundation-long-reverb-glide-overlap-pingpong-echo-two-sines-440-432-panned-lr-8hz-narrow-notch-carrier-in-music-bus-fades-gentle-limiter-export",
 "Produce a binaural-beat meditation track: a two-chord pad foundation with long reverb and overlapping glided notes, a sparse echoing piano and subtle bass, volume-automated arrangement, then a binaural layer of two pure sines (440 Hz and 432 Hz for an 8 Hz difference) panned hard left/right, a narrow high-Q EQ cut at the carrier frequency on the music bus so nothing masks it, fades, a gentle limiter (~3 dB input, no audible gain reduction) and WAV/MP3 export at 44.1 kHz",
 "Make the whole track, not just the beat. Start from pads: play a simple two-chord loop (around A and D), add reverb with high decay and wet mix, keep chord changes on the grid, and let notes overlap with glide so transitions slide instead of stepping. Add a short piano line with an echo (more feedback, ping-pong on) and its own reverb, then a subtle low bass on the same roots; pull all levels down to avoid overdrive. Arrange by drawing volume automation so elements enter and leave (less is more; one element can carry minutes), boost the highs with a high shelf on bright parts, put delay before reverb. Binaural layer: a sine at the root frequency (A = 440 Hz) and a duplicate at 432 Hz (8 Hz difference); unpanned they make an audible wobble (monaural beat), panned hard left and right they form the binaural beat. Group the music and cut a narrow notch (high Q) at 440 Hz so the instrumental doesn't overlap the carrier. Fade the beat in and out, keep it quiet but present when muted/unmuted. On the master, add a limiter on the loudest section with only ~3 dB input and no audible gain reduction; export 44.1 kHz WAV plus an MP3, then do a calm test listen later and adjust.",
 ["DAW with stock pad/piano/bass instruments, reverb, echo/delay, EQ, utility gain and limiter, or Web Audio: two OscillatorNodes (f and f-Δ) each through a StereoPannerNode at -1/+1, a BiquadFilter type 'notch' at f with high Q on the music bus, a DynamicsCompressor as gentle limiter (implementation note)","Headphones for checking the binaural layer"],
 [("root_hz","number","Carrier frequency matching the track's root (440 Hz for A in the video)",True),("beat_hz","number","Left/right difference (8 Hz in the video)",True),("chords","list","Simple pad progression (A and D in the video)",True),("length","duration","Track length",False)],
 [("meditation_track","audio","Exported WAV (44.1 kHz) and MP3"),("binaural_layer","track","Two panned sines with fades"),("arrangement","automation","Volume automation for entries/exits")],
 [HC,"Without hard left/right panning the two sines only produce a monaural wobble.","Skip the notch and other instruments around the carrier frequency mask it; cut narrowly so the music isn't thinned.","Avoid heavy compression/limiting in meditation music; no audible distortion or pumping.","Factory preset names are not needed; any soft pad, piano and bass work. Bit depth depends on the distributor."],
 [("Pick a soft pad, play a simple two-chord loop (e.g. A then D), add reverb with high decay and wet mix, capture the MIDI and align chord changes to the grid; loop it.","pad foundation","A spacious harmonic bed"),
  ("Layer a second pad for texture and a sparse piano phrase; add an echo with more feedback and ping-pong, plus a reverb with moderated wet mix.","layers","A moving echo line over the pads"),
  ("Lengthen notes so they overlap at chord changes and enable glide so pitch slides between them.","glide","Smooth gliding transitions instead of steps"),
  ("Add a subtle low bass on the roots; lower all instrument levels to avoid clipping.","bass","Grounded low end with headroom"),
  ("Arrange with volume automation: bring elements in and out gradually; high-shelf boost and mid cut on bright parts; put delay before reverb.","arrangement","A slowly evolving track that starts and ends gently"),
  ("Binaural layer: a sine instrument at 440 Hz and a duplicate at 432 Hz; listen unpanned (wobble), then pan one hard left and one hard right; program one note across the whole track.","binaural","Two pure tones forming an 8 Hz binaural beat on headphones"),
  ("Group the music tracks and add a narrow, high-Q EQ cut at 440 Hz on the group.","carrier notch","The beat sounds clean, without resonance from the music"),
  ("Fade the beat in and out; set its level so it is quiet but noticeably different when muted.","beat level","Present but unobtrusive beat"),
  ("On the master, loop the loudest section and add a limiter with about 3 dB input and no audible gain reduction; export WAV 44.1 kHz and an MP3; test-listen later in a calm setting and adjust.","master + export","Clean, gentle final files"),
 ],
 ["Left channel contains only the 440 Hz sine and right only the 432 Hz sine (check by soloing each side).","A spectrum of the music group shows a narrow dip at 440 Hz.","The limiter shows little or no gain reduction on the loudest section.","Exported files are 44.1 kHz."],
 ["binaural","meditation","ambient","pad","glide","reverb","notch-eq","mixing","mastering","mind-amend","web-audio"],
 note="Long DAW walkthrough; coaching/one-on-one call promo and project-download talk dropped; numeric values are the presenter's.")

mk("bFaYEPLlIxQ","Binaural Beats Are Boring… So I Built Something Else | Bisochronic® Studio",
 "layered-binaural-plus-isochronic-architecture-carrier-left-carrier-plus-beat-right-octave-layers-pulse-ratio-locked-to-beat-sine-pulse-pingpong-pulse-harmonic-layers-lowpass-sweep-noise-drift-drone-fifth-journey-ramps",
 "Layered binaural + isochronic track architecture: carrier in the left ear and carrier+beat in the right (e.g. 100/104 or 100/110 Hz), octave-up/down copies for depth, an isochronic pulse ratio-locked to the beat rate (1:1, 1:2, 2:1) with sine pulse shape, optional left-right ping-pong pulsing, extra harmonic/anchor tone layers pulsed at the same rate, a slow lowpass sweep that fades harmonic layers in and out, panned drifting pink/white/brown noise atmosphere, a drone a fifth below, and a 'journey' of timed beat-rate segments with smooth ramps (e.g. 10 → 1.5 Hz descent)",
 "Design richer Mind Amend-style tracks than a bare two-tone beat. Binaural: one pure tone per ear, the right ear tone = carrier + beat (100 Hz left, 104 Hz right gives a 4 Hz beat; removing one ear leaves a steady tone). Isochronic: the same rate as on/off pulsing of a tone; both can play together. Choose any comfortable carrier (100 Hz in the demo, with micro-tuning), add octave layers (½× and 2× carrier) for depth, and pulse layers at a rate locked to the beat by a simple ratio (1:1, half, double) so nothing drifts out of sync when the beat changes; sine pulse shape is preferred. Ping-pong pulse alternates the pulse left/right instead of on/off. Extra harmonic tones (the presenter uses solfeggio-labelled frequencies and says they lack scientific basis) or a fifth/12th harmonic stack are pulsed at the same rate. A lowpass sweep (period ~6 s and longer) fades harmonic layers in and out so no tone is constant. Pink/white/brown noise as wind/ocean air, auto-panned. A low drone a fifth below supports the field. A journey timeline holds a beat rate for set minutes, then glides smoothly to the next (5 Hz 5 min → 2 Hz → 1 Hz; sleep descent 10 → 1.5 Hz; a 12 → 4.5 Hz ramp). Polyrhythm Studio: one shared rate LFO drives beat offset and every pulse lane via integer ratios.",
 ["DAW (the presenter uses one) or a dedicated generator, or Web Audio: carrier OscillatorNodes per ear via ChannelMergerNode, right-ear frequency = carrier + beat(t); pulse gains from one master rate × ratio; StereoPannerNode for ping-pong (pan = sign of the pulse LFO); BiquadFilter lowpass with a slow LFO on the harmonic bus; noise buffer + slow auto-pan; beat(t) from setValueAtTime/linearRampToValueAtTime segments (implementation note)","Headphones"],
 [("carrier_hz","number","Carrier frequency (100 Hz in the demo)",True),("beat_hz","number|segments","Beat rate, or journey segments [{rate, minutes}] with smooth transitions",True),("pulse_ratio","enum","Pulse rate relative to beat: 1:1 | 1:2 | 2:1",False),("layers","list","Octave up/down, harmonic/anchor tones, fifth/12th stack, drone, noise",False),("sweep_period_s","number","Lowpass sweep period for fading harmonic layers (≈6 s and longer)",False)],
 [("layered_session","patch","Multi-layer binaural + isochronic session"),("journey","automation","Timed beat-rate segments"),("session_card","text","List of carrier, beat, ratios and layers used (for documentation)")],
 [HC,"Solfeggio-style frequencies have no scientific backing (the presenter says so); treat them as optional harmonic colour.","Keep every pulse layer on an integer ratio of the beat so layers never drift into phantom beats against each other.","Noise atmosphere can be harsh; keep it low and optional.","The video promotes the presenter's own web tool; steps here are its layer architecture rebuilt generically. No presets copied."],
 [("Set the carrier (e.g. 100 Hz pure sine) in the left ear and carrier + beat in the right (e.g. 104 Hz for 4 Hz). Check with headphones: removing one side leaves a steady tone.","binaural core","A 4 Hz binaural beat on headphones"),
  ("Add octave copies at half and double the carrier at lower level.","octave layers","More depth and bass without a new beat rate"),
  ("Add an isochronic pulse with sine shape on a layer at ratio 1:1 to the beat; try half and double. Change the beat and confirm the pulse follows.","locked pulse","Pulses stay in a fixed ratio to the beat"),
  ("Optional ping-pong: pulse the layer left/right instead of on/off and raise depth.","ping-pong","Pulse moves side to side"),
  ("Add harmonic/anchor tones (e.g. a fifth or 12th above, or chosen fixed tones) pulsed at the same rate or left steady.","harmonics","A chord-like tonal field that pulses together"),
  ("Put a slow lowpass sweep on the harmonic layers (start ~6 s period, then longer) with adjustable intensity.","sweep","Harmonic layers swell in and out instead of sounding constantly"),
  ("Add a low pink/white/brown noise layer with slow auto-pan, and a low drone a fifth below.","atmosphere + drone","Air and low support tying layers together"),
  ("Build a journey: hold each beat rate for set minutes and glide to the next (e.g. 5 Hz 5 min → 2 Hz 5 min → 1 Hz; or a 10 → 1.5 Hz descent); export and keep a card listing every frequency and ratio.","journey","The beat rate changes smoothly over the track; settings are documented")],
 ["Right-ear frequency minus left-ear frequency equals the target beat rate at every point of the journey.","Every pulse layer's rate is beat × ratio (1, ½ or 2).","Harmonic layers rise and fall over the sweep period.","Journey transitions are glides, with no sudden rate jumps."],
 ["binaural","isochronic","pulse","layering","octave","drone","noise","journey","ramps","mind-amend","polyrhythm-studio","web-audio"],
 note="Creator's tool walkthrough; channel milestones, Reaper aside and preset-submission talk dropped.")
